Although networks can diverge in weight space without increasing barriers ([Frankle et al. 2020a](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2506.13234#bib.bib18); [Vlaar & Frankle 2022](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2506.13234#bib.bib66)), we find in our experiments that barriers and L2L^{2} divergence after training exhibit a strong log-linear relationship ([Figure 7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2506.13234#S5.F7 "In Barriers scale with with exponential L 2 divergence in vision settings. ‣ 5 L 2 Divergence and Barriers ‣ The Butterfly Effect: Neural Network Training Trajectories Are Highly Sensitive to Initial Conditions") left). This finding differs from [Vlaar & Frankle 2022](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2506.13234#bib.bib66) in that they look at the distance traveled from initialization, whereas we look at the distance between training trajectories which started from the same point. We find that the proportion of identity elements in the aligning permutations PP is also related to barriers, albeit to a weaker extent ([Figure 16](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2506.13234#A3.F16 "In C.5 Fixed Points of Aligning Permutations ‣ Appendix C Further Experiments ‣ The Butterfly Effect: Neural Network Training Trajectories Are Highly Sensitive to Initial Conditions")). Since PP minimizes L2L^{2} distance between θT\theta\_{T} and θT′\theta^{\prime}\_{T}, this is likely due to the correlation between barriers and L2L^{2} divergence.

