*Extended pre-training yields near-zero fine-tuning barriers in ResNet-50/CIFAR settings.* [Figure 5](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2506.13234#S4.F5 "In 4.4 Fine-tuning ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ The Butterfly Effect: Neural Network Training Trajectories Are Highly Sensitive to Initial Conditions") shows that fine-tuning from later checkpoints greatly improves stability in ResNet-50 experiments. For improved readability, we zoom into the perturbations with σ≤10−2\sigma\leq 10^{-2} in [Figures 18](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2506.13234#A4.F18 "In D.1 ResNet Fine-Tuning ‣ Appendix D Further Fine-tuning Results ‣ The Butterfly Effect: Neural Network Training Trajectories Are Highly Sensitive to Initial Conditions"), [5](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2506.13234#A4.T5 "Table 5 ‣ D.1 ResNet Fine-Tuning ‣ Appendix D Further Fine-tuning Results ‣ The Butterfly Effect: Neural Network Training Trajectories Are Highly Sensitive to Initial Conditions") and [4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2506.13234#A4.T4 "Table 4 ‣ D.1 ResNet Fine-Tuning ‣ Appendix D Further Fine-tuning Results ‣ The Butterfly Effect: Neural Network Training Trajectories Are Highly Sensitive to Initial Conditions"), revealing that transferring from CIFAR-100 to CIFAR-10 and vice-versa results in near-zero barriers. The CIFAR-100 to CIFAR-10 direction exhibits greater stability, which could indicate that CIFAR-100 pre-training is better suited for optimization on CIFAR-10 than the other way around.

