Here, we examine the relationship between the barriers and the L2L^{2} divergence between models at the end of training in greater detail. [Figure 25](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2506.13234#A4.F25 "In D.5 L 2 Divergence and Barriers ‣ Appendix D Further Fine-tuning Results ‣ The Butterfly Effect: Neural Network Training Trajectories Are Highly Sensitive to Initial Conditions") (left, middle) shows that fine-tuning on vision tasks, such as transferring from CIFAR-100 to CIFAR-10 and vice versa, follows the trends presented in [Figure 7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2506.13234#S5.F7 "In Barriers scale with with exponential L 2 divergence in vision settings. ‣ 5 L 2 Divergence and Barriers ‣ The Butterfly Effect: Neural Network Training Trajectories Are Highly Sensitive to Initial Conditions") (left). We see that this direct relationship is weak or non-existent when transferring ViTs from ImageNet to CIFAR-100 ([Figure 25](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2506.13234#A4.F25 "In D.5 L 2 Divergence and Barriers ‣ Appendix D Further Fine-tuning Results ‣ The Butterfly Effect: Neural Network Training Trajectories Are Highly Sensitive to Initial Conditions") right), as well as for the GLUE benchmark in our study ([Figure 26](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2506.13234#A4.F26 "In D.5 L 2 Divergence and Barriers ‣ Appendix D Further Fine-tuning Results ‣ The Butterfly Effect: Neural Network Training Trajectories Are Highly Sensitive to Initial Conditions")), with QNLI and COLA exhibiting almost no correlation. Interestingly, OLMo fine-tuned on GSM8K ([Figure 24](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2506.13234#A4.F24 "In D.4 OLMo Fine-Tuning ‣ Appendix D Further Fine-tuning Results ‣ The Butterfly Effect: Neural Network Training Trajectories Are Highly Sensitive to Initial Conditions"), right) shows a clearer correlation between barriers and L2L^{2} divergence, which more closely resembles our ResNet-20 results ([Figure 7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2506.13234#